// Verbatim text: inline \verb and the verbatim environment.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/verbatim-demo.script -p letter

\font lmroman 12 regular

\bold{Verbatim text}

Inline verbatim is set with \verb|\verb|. The character right after it is the
delimiter, and the text runs to that character's next occurrence — set literally
in the typewriter face. So \verb|x = a_b + c| and a URL like
\verb|http://example.com/path| keep every character exactly as written, with no
comment, escape, or active character given its usual meaning. Pick a delimiter
the text does not use: \verb#a|b|c# uses a hash because the text contains bars.

The verbatim environment sets a whole block the same way: one source line per
output line, leading spaces preserved, and no line breaking.

\begin{verbatim}
def factorial(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n - 1)   // a // here is literal, not a comment

print(factorial(5))
\end{verbatim}

Running text resumes as an ordinary paragraph after the block, flush with the
left margin and broken normally.
